WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600
Boulder Dash, my son, you must take up the quest I never completed.

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:07.140
Find the secret jewels

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:11.140
of the six worlds, and you will be rich beyond your wildest dreams.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:34.680
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:36.640
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:37.940
And I'm Joe.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:39.760
Good to have you back, Sean.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:42.840
We'll get to that in a second because I don't want to stray

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:46.360
too far from the back of the box that I just read, which are the final-

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:48.200
It's okay. I love it. I love that I'm back.

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:50.080
I really appreciate the warm welcome.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:55.100
Those are the final words of the father of the player character in this game.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:56.720
Powerful stuff, guys.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:02.820
Very emotional. I was just going to say that immediately I am floored that there is so much plot that I

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:03.640
had no idea about.

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:06.600
From like your first sentence there, I was like, wow, this is, maybe I should

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:07.680
have read the manual of this game.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:09.220
I feel like I've been saying that a lot lately.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:10.600
That was your dad?

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:12.460
That was your dad.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:14.540
Oh, that was my dad.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:16.420
He works for Nintendo.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:19.720
It was not anybody on the show's dad, yeah.

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:22.600
It was Boulder Dash's dad?

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:24.020
Like, does the character

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:25.760
have a name or is he just Boulder Dash?

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:29.700
I guess I did just think it was me because I get to choose the color.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:31.200
No, the kid has a name.

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:33.160
His name is Rockford.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:35.320
Do we like that? Is that a good name for a

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:36.520
guy who- No, it is not a good name.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.980
It's pretty lame. Sorry to any Rockford listeners that we have.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:44.460
His father's name was Stoneford.

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:46.380
Much better name.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:49.680
So what will his son be?

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:53.100
Like, you can't get more specific.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:54.500
Pebbleford. Oh, Pebble.

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:55.600
Oh, yeah, you're getting smaller.

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:58.760
No, his father was Stone and he's Rock, so

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:01.300
his grandfather must have been Pebbleford.

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:04.000
Oh. He could be Boulderford.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:07.680
Boulderford, yeah. There's so many different names left.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.380
Yeah, you're working up to like Mountain and Planet.

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:14.620
If he finds all the secret jewels, he can be Jewel Ford.

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:16.400
Jewel Verne.

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:20.080
Or just Jewel, and you could record music into that name.

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:23.640
Rockford's son is Harrison Ford.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:25.560
Oh, that's right.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:26.980
That does make sense.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:30.320
Yeah, this is part of the American Graffiti Cinematic Universe.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:33.140
Okay, this is way too silly.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:34.900
We need to start talking about the game.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:39.100
And, you know, I just wanted to point out before we actually start into the game, just

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:42.480
to lay some, like, I guess, developer talk or whatever.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:46.140
It's been a while since we've had this kind of game on the show.

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:55.960
And not like a game where you dig through rocks to find jewels or anything, but just a game that was first released in 1984,

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:01.120
only, like, for computers or whatever, developed by just two guys.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:10.900
You know, we saw a lot of these in, like, 87, I want to say, and 86, where it was, like, you know, one guy or a small group of guys just, like, said,

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:12.780
yeah, screw it, I'll make video games.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:20.220
And I'm thinking of, like, Lode Runner, Raid on Bungling Bay, Spelunker, all of those, like, Broderbund releases.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:26.740
This was published by JVC Musical Industries, which is different than Broderbund.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:31.960
It doesn't sound like they're even interested in video games based on the musical industries.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:42.380
But, you know, just wanted to throw that out there, that this is maybe got to be judged with a different lens when you realize only two guys made it instead of, like, a team at Namco.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:52.040
Well, even that whole publisher thing, where it's like, wait, so you're saying that Lean Cuisine released this video game?

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:56.460
Like, that whole concept of, like, they don't traditionally do this?

00:03:56.540 --> 00:04:02.580
Like, I feel like we saw a lot of that in, like, the 88, like, the 87, 88, like you were saying.

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:06.520
So, yeah, I noticed that.

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:11.900
Sean, I gotta ask you, because, you know, that joke, you know, it's a good joke,

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:17.600
but Lean Cuisine is probably the last words I could have expected that you would say there in that joke.

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:18.760
Where did that come from?

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:22.140
Um, I don't know.

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:25.760
I haven't eaten Lean Cuisine in three years.

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:29.800
Well, you know what? You could have been channeling it, and I'm not speaking for you here,

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:38.640
but I know that, like, the Kid Cuisine used to, like, occasionally come with, like, a CD-ROM disc if you bought it.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:39.500
No, it didn't.

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:41.260
Yeah, it did. There was all sorts of things.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:44.980
The cereal boxes used to come with, like, CD-ROM video games.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:47.540
I had, like, a Cocoa Puffs video game on my computer.

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:54.380
It was like any other red-blooded American, but I didn't know that Kid's Cuisine did that.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:55.560
You know, the guy with the penguin?

00:04:56.440 --> 00:05:00.160
The funny thing is, when you said Lean Cuisine, I was thinking Kid Cuisine.

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:03.060
So it's even crazier to me that Kid's Cuisine actually did do that,

00:05:03.140 --> 00:05:04.960
because that's the one I thought was the most random.

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:08.440
We're still on topic, right?

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:10.800
Yeah. I think we're doing really good.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:11.980
In fact, you know what?

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:18.020
Apparently, this is just a joke, of course, but there is a mock-up.

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:21.860
If you type in Kid Cuisine video game, there's a mock-up for a

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:28.140
Kid Cuisine goes to Washington Sega CD game that does not exist, but is very funny,

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:33.280
and it's Kid Cuisine saying lobby for flavor, lobby for fun, lobby for kids,

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:36.800
and I like that they chose the Sega CD as the platform of choice.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:39.280
And lobbying as, like, what you do.

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:43.740
Well, that's very reminiscent of, like, you know, Yoshi commits tax evasion or

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:45.020
whatever, or tax fraud.

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:46.440
As one does.

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:50.520
Back onto Boulder Dash, you know, because I know we've already talked so much

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:54.860
about this game and covered so much of it, but just to, you know, maybe refresh

00:05:54.860 --> 00:06:00.760
everybody's memory, Boulder Dash is, like, in, I guess it's like an arcade-style

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:05.720
game, very reminiscent of Dig Dug, where stages take place in caves.

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:08.780
In each cave, you're kind of just...

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:14.760
You're walking through them, but theoretically, if, you know, if a little animation was on

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:15.680
the screen, it's not.

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:18.960
But if a little animation was on screen, you might see, like, a pickaxe or something as

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:23.820
you chip away at the cave and reveal new areas.

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:30.820
And inside this top-down view of this cave, there are sometimes enemies, sometimes boulders,

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:34.740
sometimes animals, and mostly jewels.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:37.880
That is the object of choice, the object to collect.

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:44.120
Once you collect the amount of jewels required, it's in the top left of the screen, a door will

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:45.180
appear for you to exit.

00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:49.260
Now, you're free to collect more jewels or just, you know, continue to clean the cave if

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:50.240
you're into that kind of thing.

00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:51.580
But that's all you have to do.

00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:56.400
Collect all the jewels that are required of you, leave the stage, move on to the next stage.

00:06:56.660 --> 00:07:01.720
And surprisingly, it goes a little step beyond what we're used to in arcade-style games by

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:07.960
actually having a world map that you can select the level, and you don't have to necessarily

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:12.520
go right to the stage 1-1 at the start of the game.

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:16.560
I didn't end on a thought there, so do you want me to give you a thought?

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:18.120
Like, do you want to react to that?

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:27.840
No, okay, so it's funny that you call this an arcade-style game, and I guess by classic

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:37.420
definitions it is, but this seems much more methodical than most arcade games.

00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:38.900
Like, this is a puzzle game.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:45.080
Would we consider, like, Lode Runner an arcade game?

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:46.380
Yeah, it's a good point.

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:54.600
You're right. Puzzle game, I guess I struggle with just because there is some puzzling, I suppose, but, you

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:57.580
know, it's really more like try not to softlock yourself.

00:07:57.880 --> 00:07:59.900
Oh, I'm surprised you say that, because I...

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:00.780
I mean, that's 100% a...

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:02.560
This is 100% a puzzle game.

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:04.320
Yeah, I was actually...

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:07.580
I went through, like, a weird kind of rollercoaster when I started this.

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:11.960
I was like... I saw the world map, and I was like, this is going to be, like...

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:13.540
There's going to be a lot of love put in this game.

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:15.260
It's going to be, like, a really cool...

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:17.260
I think I had an idea that it was a puzzle game.

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:18.660
I think I saw footage of it or something.

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:21.220
So, like, a really cool puzzle game.

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:23.580
Then I started the first couple levels, and I was like, oh, okay.

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:24.800
It's one of those things that's, like...

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:25.760
They're, like, non-puzzles.

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:28.360
They're just, like... It's just, like, collect all the stuff, and you're done.

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:34.740
And then I kept playing, and I was like, oh, there actually are some very interestingly designed puzzle elements in it.

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:40.780
Not that they get too complicated, but they did feel above and beyond what we're used to in this style of game.

00:08:40.900 --> 00:08:42.480
And I felt like there were...

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:45.620
I didn't beat the game, but in the ones that I got through, there were some...

00:08:45.620 --> 00:08:48.420
Some that I considered fun, interesting puzzles.

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:51.320
Yeah, like, each of these...

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:56.840
Like, the cast of, I guess, blocks sort of expands as you play.

00:08:56.840 --> 00:09:04.480
Okay? Just starting with boulders and the aforementioned jewels that you're trying to collect.

00:09:04.780 --> 00:09:09.460
And then there's, like, other moving obstacles.

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:13.680
And then there's ones that interact slightly differently.

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:16.940
That, like, this one jumps around, and this one just goes in a circle.

00:09:16.940 --> 00:09:18.360
And, like, whenever...

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:20.800
This one, if you let it go, it will...

00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:27.900
It will sort of very aggressively track you down.

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:30.400
And this one will just sort of loll around for a bit.

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:33.920
Like, the difference between the ghosts and Pac-Man.

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:37.960
And this sort of block will...

00:09:37.960 --> 00:09:39.720
Like, the game has its own weird physics.

00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:42.900
And this sort of block will stack this way before, like...

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:44.000
Before falling over.

00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:46.620
And this sort of block will have a little bit more structure.

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:54.060
So, there's a lot going on here with a pretty simple method of interaction.

00:09:54.400 --> 00:10:00.040
Yeah, and it encourages you to learn those physics.

00:10:00.100 --> 00:10:01.740
Which I'm sure we'll get into later.

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:06.040
I have something that clicked in my head that made me suddenly, like...

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:08.140
Very intuitively understand the physics.

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:10.680
Whereas, originally, it was very difficult to understand.

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:14.080
But once you learn those physics, it makes you kind of use them to your advantage.

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:16.640
To, like, make things fall into certain places.

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:18.080
Or, like, some areas, like...

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:20.400
Oh, you have to fill in this area with boulders.

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:24.840
So, you gotta, like, kind of use the game's physics to make an avalanche.

00:10:24.860 --> 00:10:26.480
So, these boulders will all land here.

00:10:26.620 --> 00:10:31.940
And in that sense, there's a lot of interesting thought processes that have to happen.

00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:33.700
Joe, you mentioned the physics.

00:10:33.700 --> 00:10:36.120
But I'm curious, just from a...

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:40.540
You know, and it's possible to understand it as you're playing the game.

00:10:40.820 --> 00:10:47.540
But, graphically, there is something going on here, at least in my head, about, like, how gravity works in these caves.

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:51.240
Yes. Because it would appear that either one of two things is happening.

00:10:51.380 --> 00:10:54.380
Either the cave is on, like, a 45-degree slant.

00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:55.780
Or, like, you're...

00:10:55.780 --> 00:11:03.820
When you're going north on the screen, you're climbing up the slant and things are falling down as you move them.

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:06.080
Or, it's like, who really cares?

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:15.860
And, you know, Rockford is climbing around a cave that otherwise is just, like, you're viewing the side of the cave from this top-down perspective.

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:17.900
It's just very confusing.

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:25.320
This, along with just even some visuals, like I was talking about, it'd be nice if there was just, like, a little pickaxe animation every time he went through these rocks.

00:11:25.320 --> 00:11:31.960
It just feels like it's missing a little context into what he's doing inside these caves.

00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:35.220
And from a gameplay perspective, it's not screwing anything up.

00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:37.760
But, you know, it's a little strange.

00:11:38.420 --> 00:11:46.500
Yeah, that kind of perspective issue you mentioned was the biggest reason that it took me a while to understand what was going on with the physics.

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:47.900
It's like Dig Dug, right?

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:49.940
Like, it's sort of in the same vein as that.

00:11:50.260 --> 00:11:55.300
Yeah, but Dig Dug, if I remember correctly, it's clear that you're going up and down, right?

00:11:55.640 --> 00:11:59.900
Yes, you're going down from the ground, you know, so you're digging into the ground.

00:11:59.900 --> 00:12:07.140
Right. Based on the way this sprite actually moves, to me, looked like, okay, I'm going north, south, east, and west.

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:10.720
But then everything else moves as if down is down.

00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:18.240
So I had to just eventually, like, be like, okay, I'm, even though the sprite looks funky, I'm moving up and down somehow.

00:12:18.440 --> 00:12:20.520
Yeah, you gotta have to. I'm climbing, I'm rappelling, or whatever.

00:12:20.520 --> 00:12:22.540
But I mean, like, okay.

00:12:22.540 --> 00:12:30.340
Okay. I wouldn't say that it broke my brain trying to visualize that.

00:12:30.460 --> 00:12:34.900
It's pretty intuitive, like, in terms of just the whole gravity situation.

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:48.680
I guess where the physics start to be, like, you gotta think about it more, is how things sort of stack on top of each other, and how stable those stacks are.

00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:49.720
Right, exactly. Exactly.

00:12:50.060 --> 00:12:51.720
And I think we agree with you on that, Sean.

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:59.400
We were saying that, you know, you're able to play the game without having to think about that, but if you really think about how gravity works in these caves, it doesn't make any sense.

00:13:00.040 --> 00:13:05.580
Well, if you think about how pipes work in Mario, like, what is this place?

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:08.380
Is it just, like, a place with magic plumbing?

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:14.660
No, I read the April issue of Nintendo Power 2001, where they explained how warped pipes work.

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:16.860
So, you know, I'm very familiar with that.

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:21.900
Yeah, it also is, I mean, even if it's not, yeah, it's not super, super confusing, you get it right away.

00:13:21.900 --> 00:13:31.760
It's a little jarring at first to be like, I'm walking this way, but think, because it's not immediately clear that, like, you walk through something and everything's going to fall.

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:39.580
So it is a little, like, I do think it's an oversight on their part, even if it's, even if it, you know, you can figure it out.

00:13:39.720 --> 00:13:44.200
Like, it's just, they have two different perspectives that don't match up.

00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:50.800
So how hard is it to complete the goal of the stage, in which case collect diamonds, right?

00:13:51.560 --> 00:14:02.460
Don't focus on whether that's interesting or not, but just how challenging do these stages get, given that the diamonds aren't always obviously shown on screen, right?

00:14:02.540 --> 00:14:08.460
Like, the first stage, you're given the idea that, oh, I'll always know where the diamonds are, and I just have to figure out how to get to them.

00:14:08.460 --> 00:14:18.100
Other times, it's like, no, I have to create some new circumstance that reveals the diamonds, or I have to kill a certain enemy to spawn some diamonds.

00:14:18.100 --> 00:14:20.220
It's not always the same in each stage.

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:31.440
Yeah, I mean, it depends on how it decides to present itself to you, because, yeah, that first level where there are no diamonds on the screen,

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:39.100
it's just those little squares where you kind of have to crush the, whatever they are.

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:46.300
It's also, what exactly the enemies are is sort of unclear at times, too.

00:14:46.480 --> 00:14:53.940
But I think it was, it only took me a few minutes to realize that that's what the game wanted me to do.

00:14:56.300 --> 00:15:03.820
So, I mean, the difficulty definitely fluctuates, but it's never like, well, how are they supposed to, how was I supposed to know that, you know?

00:15:04.140 --> 00:15:10.180
Yeah, I think that there's, like, a variety of, like, of, like, ways that you need to figure it out.

00:15:10.280 --> 00:15:16.100
Like, there's that one that you're describing where it's like, okay, you can kind of figure out, like, okay, you kill this thing,

00:15:16.220 --> 00:15:20.300
and it'll leave a diamond behind, or whatever, a grid of diamonds behind.

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:26.440
Then there are some where it's like, you really have to look at the level for a long time and, like, experiment a little bit.

00:15:26.920 --> 00:15:30.820
There's one early on, well, maybe it's in, like, the second world or the third world.

00:15:30.940 --> 00:15:35.540
No, it's the second world, where there's all these, like, amoebas at the bottom of the screen.

00:15:36.100 --> 00:15:40.540
And, like, you just, there's just nothing else for you to do, so eventually you just figure, like,

00:15:40.560 --> 00:15:45.000
oh, let me try stopping these amoebas from spreading, because every couple seconds they spread.

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:51.740
And once you, like, have them fully trapped by mammoths, which just sounds silly, because we haven't described the mammoths yet,

00:15:51.760 --> 00:15:56.380
but, like, once you have them fully trapped by mammoths, which in this world are just your version of the boulders,

00:15:57.520 --> 00:16:02.340
then once they can't spread any further and they've filled up all the space, they'll just turn into diamonds.

00:16:03.280 --> 00:16:12.380
Yeah, or the one where they, like, you have to, like, create a field that, like, boulders will pass through,

00:16:12.380 --> 00:16:16.200
and on the other side of this field, now they're diamonds.

00:16:17.020 --> 00:16:21.520
Right, yeah. Like, it is kind of stuff that's, like, well, how was I supposed to know that?

00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:26.200
But it's also stuff that, like, it feels like they're, it's intentionally designed that, like, yeah, you're not supposed to know it,

00:16:26.260 --> 00:16:28.540
but you can figure it out by experimenting.

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:34.520
And then on the last thing, there are also puzzles that it's, like, I know what I have to do,

00:16:34.620 --> 00:16:36.800
but it's really hard to not get trapped.

00:16:36.980 --> 00:16:39.540
Like, you have to really stop and think about, like, how can I get over there?

00:16:39.600 --> 00:16:41.820
How can I get over here? And that's just the ones that I played.

00:16:41.820 --> 00:16:47.040
Maybe they'll get even further, but I did appreciate that it changed it up a little bit, level to level.

00:16:47.320 --> 00:16:52.740
Yeah, it's great that it's not just, okay, here's nine diamonds, and they're across the map,

00:16:52.780 --> 00:16:56.040
and you just have to figure out how to dig towards them, and that they give you other,

00:16:56.240 --> 00:16:57.840
I think that keeps the game fresh.

00:16:58.440 --> 00:17:05.240
It's funny, because I do think that the hardest one for me was, like, crushing enemies with rocks.

00:17:05.240 --> 00:17:11.480
It required the most kind of thought, because if you let them loose, right, they're usually in, like, square boxes,

00:17:11.480 --> 00:17:14.860
or at least roaming in an area that you haven't touched yet.

00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:19.260
Once you let them loose, now it's like, you know, you don't have any way to defend yourself.

00:17:19.420 --> 00:17:23.160
There's no, like, press the B button to throw a rock at them or something.

00:17:23.340 --> 00:17:25.220
Like, you just have to hope that they don't touch you.

00:17:25.220 --> 00:17:32.580
So you really got to make sure that your placement of the rocks or the mammoths or the skulls,

00:17:32.600 --> 00:17:34.240
it changes in each world, essentially.

00:17:34.680 --> 00:17:36.980
I think eventually it's Moai heads, too.

00:17:37.180 --> 00:17:40.320
So it's just another example of an NES game with Moai heads.

00:17:41.020 --> 00:17:45.280
All of these things, I think, like, that was, to me, the most challenging one.

00:17:45.300 --> 00:17:48.220
And I think what makes it even more challenging, but wouldn't have been challenging,

00:17:48.460 --> 00:17:51.360
along with a lot of the stuff that we've been covering so far in this episode,

00:17:51.360 --> 00:17:57.200
is that if you just read the manual, a lot of this stuff is covered, you know, without difficulty.

00:17:57.760 --> 00:18:02.280
It's just very matter-of-fact about things, about, like, which enemies turn into diamonds

00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:07.780
and what each, like, type of stage objective would be

00:18:07.780 --> 00:18:14.500
and a little bit of, like, pro tips on the amoebas and stuff that you might not necessarily, like, think of

00:18:14.500 --> 00:18:17.000
unless, you know, like Joe was saying, you just kind of have this moment of, like,

00:18:17.040 --> 00:18:20.780
well, I should stop them from growing, but, like, the manual just spells all that out for you.

00:18:20.780 --> 00:18:27.240
All that to say, I think, like, it's a clearly defined game without any sort of tutorials.

00:18:27.500 --> 00:18:30.580
You know, like, the stages itself, like, they just throw you into them,

00:18:30.660 --> 00:18:33.000
but you immediately kind of get the objective.

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:36.360
Even the one that Sean was talking about, stage 1-4, I think,

00:18:36.720 --> 00:18:38.820
which is the first stage I went to because I was like,

00:18:38.840 --> 00:18:40.960
oh, this is neat, it lets you skip around in the world map.

00:18:41.200 --> 00:18:46.740
I didn't have any idea what I was doing because there were no diamonds present on that screen,

00:18:46.740 --> 00:18:51.560
but eventually, either, you know, through looking at the manual or just through some, you know,

00:18:51.900 --> 00:18:56.540
trial and error by the player, you'll figure out, like, yeah, maybe I should try to drop one of these boulders

00:18:56.540 --> 00:18:58.760
on one of these enemies and then they'll give you the diamonds.

00:18:59.240 --> 00:19:03.160
Yeah, and just to second what you were saying there about the, I feel like,

00:19:03.160 --> 00:19:06.320
I feel like anything that involves enemies or whether there's enemies running around the stage,

00:19:06.560 --> 00:19:10.660
you are just, on top of this, like, minor puzzle solving, you're in constant danger.

00:19:10.900 --> 00:19:13.920
Like, it's very hard to predict the enemies.

00:19:14.140 --> 00:19:18.000
Granted, like, they all have a set pattern and you can stop before you let them out

00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:19.520
and, like, try and figure out where they're going to go,

00:19:19.580 --> 00:19:20.780
but everything's happening so fast.

00:19:20.780 --> 00:19:22.660
I feel it's very dangerous.

00:19:24.740 --> 00:19:32.940
I am glad to hear that the manual does sort of clearly define this stuff because,

00:19:33.280 --> 00:19:37.740
I mean, it is fine and it is acceptable if this is all stuff you have to figure out on your own,

00:19:38.980 --> 00:19:44.220
but the fact that it's clearly defined, the rules are easy to follow,

00:19:44.220 --> 00:19:49.640
I appreciate that it wasn't just, like, a kid would have to figure that out, too.

00:19:49.640 --> 00:19:53.720
We are people that are well-versed in puzzle games and we figured it out,

00:19:53.880 --> 00:19:57.440
but maybe Jimmy won't be able to.

00:20:01.120 --> 00:20:05.500
It reminds me of the Windows game Rodent's Revenge.

00:20:05.880 --> 00:20:07.120
I don't know if you guys ever played that one.

00:20:07.200 --> 00:20:14.500
That's the one where you're the mouse and you move around green blocks to try to trap cats that are free.

00:20:14.500 --> 00:20:20.380
It looks exactly like Boulder Dash in the sense that there is a, it's like a cave-like looking structure

00:20:20.380 --> 00:20:26.260
and instead of clearing the blocks, you're actually pushing the blocks to kind of create these areas

00:20:26.260 --> 00:20:29.760
where the cats can no longer roam free.

00:20:29.980 --> 00:20:31.860
And so you're doing the opposite, you know.

00:20:31.960 --> 00:20:35.360
Instead of unleashing the enemies like you do in Boulder Dash,

00:20:35.360 --> 00:20:39.720
in that game you would try to do what you do to the amoebas

00:20:39.720 --> 00:20:44.900
and kind of suffocate the enemies in order to move on to each stage.

00:20:45.060 --> 00:20:48.740
And so I'm sure Boulder Dash came first,

00:20:48.740 --> 00:20:50.600
and so now I've never thought of it this way before,

00:20:50.680 --> 00:20:53.120
but Rodent's Revenge is a reverse Boulder Dash game.

00:20:53.540 --> 00:20:57.360
Cute. So without giving your Essential Games list vote,

00:20:58.600 --> 00:21:02.980
you know, how replayable is Boulder Dash in the sense of like,

00:21:03.100 --> 00:21:06.900
how satisfying is it to collect these jewels?

00:21:06.900 --> 00:21:13.800
Is it a soothing experience to kind of complete a stage and say like,

00:21:13.880 --> 00:21:15.820
oh, that was nice, what's one more stage?

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:19.280
Like, does it have that one more play factor to it?

00:21:19.440 --> 00:21:20.320
I don't know.

00:21:20.880 --> 00:21:24.800
I mean, a lot of, most puzzle games aren't really replayable.

00:21:24.980 --> 00:21:31.080
Like, even the gold standard in the modern era of like Portal or Portal 2,

00:21:31.240 --> 00:21:34.280
like, you probably have to give yourself a few years before you can,

00:21:35.140 --> 00:21:38.300
before you can even replay it for the story.

00:21:38.440 --> 00:21:40.560
Because, you know, once you've figured out a puzzle,

00:21:41.240 --> 00:21:44.520
and the puzzle is well designed, it's probably going to be pretty memorable.

00:21:45.620 --> 00:21:51.480
It's hard to feel engaged while you're replaying that, at least in my opinion.

00:21:51.740 --> 00:21:54.360
So I don't know how replayable any of this stuff is.

00:21:54.860 --> 00:21:58.160
Yeah, I mean, I guess if, yeah, if that's what you mean by replayable,

00:21:58.320 --> 00:21:59.620
then like, I totally agree.

00:21:59.800 --> 00:22:01.640
But then you were asking a little bit about like,

00:22:02.140 --> 00:22:04.860
how much does it make you want to move on to the next stage?

00:22:06.200 --> 00:22:10.120
And I found, you know, it didn't not do that.

00:22:10.280 --> 00:22:13.020
It wasn't like, I wasn't like I was always itching to get to the next stage.

00:22:13.100 --> 00:22:14.980
But I was always like, oh, I could do one more like that.

00:22:15.240 --> 00:22:20.620
I guess I was, I was a little surprised at how satisfied I was with a lot of these stages

00:22:20.620 --> 00:22:23.280
based on like my initial impression when I first booted it up.

00:22:23.500 --> 00:22:24.660
I would agree with you, Joe.

00:22:24.800 --> 00:22:29.400
I think there's short enough bursts that it did kind of trick you that like,

00:22:29.680 --> 00:22:33.140
once you were able to solve it, and it just brings you to the next stage,

00:22:33.140 --> 00:22:35.420
you're like, sure, like, you know, let me try this one.

00:22:35.460 --> 00:22:39.120
And I kept playing until I got stuck and then was just like, well, I'm done with that.

00:22:39.220 --> 00:22:43.420
So I also agree with Sean that like, there is a point where you're not going to go back

00:22:43.420 --> 00:22:45.280
to the ones you solved, like you solve them.

00:22:45.440 --> 00:22:50.140
Now, you know, the solution, or I guess some some of the earlier stages that aren't,

00:22:51.100 --> 00:22:54.520
you know, that are a little more open to interpretation of how you would solve them

00:22:54.520 --> 00:22:57.440
or super easy to solve are don't run into that problem.

00:22:58.280 --> 00:23:03.040
All that to say, you know, yes, the question was two different

00:23:03.040 --> 00:23:10.100
questions. But I think that, you know, when you look at Wrecking Crew and Excitebike,

00:23:10.340 --> 00:23:16.000
two like, you know, NES launch games that had stage editors in them,

00:23:16.180 --> 00:23:19.780
it's a little weird that like no other game has really no other puzzle game has

00:23:19.780 --> 00:23:20.980
quite done that yet.

00:23:20.980 --> 00:23:24.720
And that this kind of game would benefit from from that kind of thing,

00:23:24.820 --> 00:23:27.680
especially for someone like Joe, who would go on and make their own puzzles.

00:23:28.700 --> 00:23:35.240
You know, just just some kind of, you know, way to not build your own stages necessarily,

00:23:35.240 --> 00:23:37.560
but to continue to generate these kinds of stages.

00:23:37.560 --> 00:23:41.480
It's just a it's a very short lived game without it.

00:23:41.720 --> 00:23:50.020
Yeah, I don't think this is the kind of game that there really is that much like there isn't

00:23:50.020 --> 00:23:53.560
a lot of like approach this from a different angle.

00:23:53.560 --> 00:23:55.220
Like this isn't an immersive sim.

00:23:55.480 --> 00:24:02.040
This is not a Deus Ex where all of the problems can be like, oh, you could take this kind of

00:24:02.040 --> 00:24:09.380
route or you could you could go like guns blazing or you can just really find some other third

00:24:09.380 --> 00:24:16.020
option. Like it's not that these are puzzles with solutions and maybe the exact way that you solve

00:24:16.020 --> 00:24:17.100
them can change.

00:24:17.100 --> 00:24:26.300
Maybe there's some wiggle room in order of operations, but it's not like it's not essentially

00:24:26.300 --> 00:24:50.660
different. Possibly why I referred to this as an arcade game is because allegedly it is the very first

00:24:50.660 --> 00:24:54.080
computer game to be converted to an arcade game.

00:24:54.080 --> 00:25:00.700
It was licensed by Exity for use with their Max of Flex arcade cabinet.

00:25:01.180 --> 00:25:03.440
And it was released in 1984.

00:25:04.180 --> 00:25:05.920
You put in a quarter.

00:25:05.920 --> 00:25:09.760
It gets you, I think, 30 seconds of game time.

00:25:10.040 --> 00:25:15.880
And then I suppose no, but I suppose like then in game you do like the jewels or something

00:25:15.880 --> 00:25:17.600
like earn you more time.

00:25:17.600 --> 00:25:20.220
But, you know, you're essentially like racing against a clock.

00:25:20.220 --> 00:25:26.160
I guess that makes sense since it's not really like it's either you solve the puzzle or you

00:25:26.160 --> 00:25:31.460
don't. And so, you know, you're running out of time, not necessarily like you could just sit there

00:25:31.460 --> 00:25:35.720
and stall and think about how you want to take on the enemies before they ever like attack

00:25:35.720 --> 00:25:42.260
you. So I could imagine that like from an arcade owner's POV, like how do I get people to put

00:25:42.260 --> 00:25:45.440
in quarters? It's like, well, make it time based instead.

00:25:45.800 --> 00:25:48.400
And I'm sure it's not the only game to do that.

00:25:48.660 --> 00:25:49.560
Yes. Yeah, it does.

00:25:51.020 --> 00:25:55.460
I'm sure that there's other examples of that, though, where like your your quarters buying

00:25:55.460 --> 00:26:00.780
like minutes of time instead of, you know, three lives.

00:26:01.040 --> 00:26:05.460
I can't think of anything off the top of my head, but that seems pretty logical in terms

00:26:05.460 --> 00:26:08.420
of how to extract money from you.

00:26:08.420 --> 00:26:13.140
It's funny because I never thought of like what it's like to from the arcade owners POV

00:26:13.140 --> 00:26:17.400
of like quarters per hour or something per cabinet or whatever.

00:26:17.580 --> 00:26:19.880
And they're like, this one's not bringing in the money anymore.

00:26:19.880 --> 00:26:24.440
So unplug it and, you know, like send it back to the distributor or whatever.

00:26:24.540 --> 00:26:25.720
Like, I don't know how that stuff works.

00:26:25.860 --> 00:26:27.420
It'd be interesting to hear from somebody.

00:26:27.960 --> 00:26:33.820
But it's like DPS, but instead it's like ROI.

00:26:34.840 --> 00:26:40.920
Whoa. Well, even even so, though, it's like, you know, there's some there's some arcade games

00:26:40.920 --> 00:26:44.840
that obviously like eight quarters like crazy.

00:26:45.380 --> 00:26:49.760
And yeah, I mean, like they're difficult by today's video game standards, but they weren't

00:26:49.760 --> 00:26:52.340
even at the time considered like unfair.

00:26:52.720 --> 00:26:55.460
Like think about Pac-Man Space Invaders, right?

00:26:55.540 --> 00:27:00.180
It was like those things ate a ton of quarters, but they didn't like rush you for your time and

00:27:00.180 --> 00:27:03.100
you didn't like just get 45 seconds.

00:27:03.100 --> 00:27:06.800
Like Sean, what was that game we played at Barcade recently where like, I don't know,

00:27:06.880 --> 00:27:09.840
we gave like a dollar 50 and we played for like two minutes.

00:27:10.280 --> 00:27:14.420
Oh, God. I, I can see it, but I don't remember what it was.

00:27:14.540 --> 00:27:16.000
Yeah, it was. It was a shooter game.

00:27:16.080 --> 00:27:17.980
I can't remember. We definitely were holding guns.

00:27:18.820 --> 00:27:19.840
Fake ones, of course.

00:27:20.340 --> 00:27:21.920
Yeah. It was like gun games.

00:27:22.040 --> 00:27:27.780
I don't remember. But they definitely have difficulty settings that can make it unfair.

00:27:28.440 --> 00:27:29.260
Yeah. The difficulty settings.

00:27:29.360 --> 00:27:32.980
I'm glad you brought that up. I don't know how this became an arcade episode, but yeah, that's definitely a thing.

00:27:32.980 --> 00:27:36.640
Even with like Pac-Man, you'll notice that a lot of Pac-Man machines are just set to be

00:27:36.640 --> 00:27:38.520
the fastest speed from the beginning.

00:27:38.520 --> 00:27:41.640
And you're like, this is not how I remember my Pac-Man.

00:27:41.840 --> 00:27:43.100
Like, did he always move this fast?

00:27:43.520 --> 00:27:45.820
Did the ghost always be this out to get you?

00:27:47.440 --> 00:27:48.360
In this arcade?

00:27:48.360 --> 00:27:54.220
In this arcade, yes. On the sequels and spinoffs side, we have had games where we're pretty into them.

00:27:55.600 --> 00:27:58.060
And, you know, we're like, oh, the sequels and spinoffs.

00:27:58.140 --> 00:27:58.960
And it's like, no, there are none.

00:27:59.300 --> 00:28:03.980
And then there are games where, you know, it's like, oh, the first time I'm ever hearing

00:28:03.980 --> 00:28:08.080
of this franchise. And it turns out that there's a few sequels and spinoffs.

00:28:08.680 --> 00:28:10.660
Is this the first time you guys are hearing of Boulder Dash?

00:28:11.220 --> 00:28:12.160
Yes. Yeah, I think so.

00:28:12.720 --> 00:28:15.440
There are 22 sequels and spinoffs.

00:28:15.680 --> 00:28:17.340
That's officially too many.

00:28:17.340 --> 00:28:21.960
So give me a number and I'll tell you about the game.

