Eight. Okay.

00:28:24.380 --> 00:28:31.960
Eight is Boulder Dash 1990 for the Game Boy, which the only anecdote I have here is that

00:28:31.960 --> 00:28:39.960
the magazine Mean Machines gave the Game Boy port of Boulder Dash a score of 90%, praising

00:28:39.960 --> 00:28:43.840
it as one of the finest video games ever written.

00:28:44.440 --> 00:28:49.160
And ever written makes me wonder, like, is there a plot in that one?

00:28:49.280 --> 00:28:52.120
Or are they talking about, like, from a code POV?

00:28:52.320 --> 00:28:53.660
You know? I mean, that is...

00:28:54.720 --> 00:28:58.020
So that's what I read on the Wikipedia for this one.

00:28:58.020 --> 00:28:59.980
Um, but I guess it was just...

00:28:59.980 --> 00:29:01.920
Maybe it was a page for the series as a whole.

00:29:02.080 --> 00:29:05.400
And I do remember that whole written thing being weird.

00:29:06.000 --> 00:29:08.240
Um, I would imagine they mean the code.

00:29:08.380 --> 00:29:09.560
And this was...

00:29:09.560 --> 00:29:11.840
This is more akin to, like, a...

00:29:11.840 --> 00:29:14.540
Like, oh, you have to write a game.

00:29:14.740 --> 00:29:15.860
You don't just...

00:29:15.860 --> 00:29:17.560
You don't design games.

00:29:17.720 --> 00:29:19.860
That's... That's not really in common parlance yet.

00:29:20.020 --> 00:29:21.320
Probably not. I'm just...

00:29:21.320 --> 00:29:22.160
I'm just guessing.

00:29:22.980 --> 00:29:27.600
Unless the Game Boy version of Boulder Dash is just, like...

00:29:27.600 --> 00:29:31.520
Is just, like, our generation's greatest story that we're all missing out on.

